# TASK A3 – DESIGN WITH POINTERS

## A3.1 – Pointer Operations (2 Marks)

Given:

```c
int stock[8];
int *p = stock;
```

The array contains 8 integer elements. The pointer `p` stores the address of the first element of the array.

### Explanation of Pointer Expressions

| Expression | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `p + 3` | Address of the fourth array element. |
| `*(p + 3)` | Value stored in the fourth array element. |
| `p[3]` | Another way to access the fourth array element. |
| `*p++` | Accesses the current value pointed to by p, then increments the pointer. |

**Important:** `*p++` means `*(p++)`. It does not increment the value stored in the array.

### Memory Diagram

Assume the starting address is 1000 and each integer occupies 4 bytes.

```text
       Array: stock[8]

 Address       Element       Pointer
 +------+      +---------+
 | 1000 | ---> | stock[0]| <--- p
 +------+      +---------+
 | 1004 |      | stock[1]|
 +------+      +---------+
 | 1008 |      | stock[2]|
 +------+      +---------+
 | 1012 |      | stock[3]| <--- p + 3
 +------+      +---------+
 | 1016 |      | stock[4]|
 +------+      +---------+
 | 1020 |      | stock[5]|
 +------+      +---------+
 | 1024 |      | stock[6]|
 +------+      +---------+
 | 1028 |      | stock[7]|
 +------+      +---------+
```

Therefore:

- `p + 3` gives the address of `stock[3]`.
- `*(p + 3)` gives the value of `stock[3]`.
- `p[3]` also gives the value of `stock[3]`.
- `*p++` reads the current element and moves the pointer to the next element.

## A3.2 – Pointers and Functions (2 Marks)

A function can find the blood group with the lowest stock and return a pointer to that stock element.

### C Function

```c
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct {
    char bloodGroup[4];
    int units;
} Stock;

Stock *findLowestStock(Stock stock[], int count)
{
    int i;
    int minIndex = 0;

    if (count <= 0)
        return NULL;

    for (i = 1; i < count; i++) {
        if (stock[i].units < stock[minIndex].units) {
            minIndex = i;
        }
    }

    return &stock[minIndex];
}
```

### Example of Calling the Function

```c
Stock *lowest = findLowestStock(stock, 8);

if (lowest != NULL) {
    printf("Lowest stock: %s\n", lowest->bloodGroup);
    lowest->units = lowest->units + 5;
}
```

The function returns the address of the stock element with the lowest number of units. The caller can update that element directly.

### Why Returning a Pointer to a Local Variable Is Wrong

A local variable exists only while its function is executing. After the function returns, the local variable's lifetime ends.

Returning its address creates a dangling pointer. Accessing it later causes undefined behaviour.

**Correct design:** Return a pointer to an element of the array passed by the caller, provided that the array remains valid.

## A3.3 – Command-Line Interface (1 Mark)

The program can accept commands using `argc` and `argv`.

### Example Commands

```text
bloodbank find O+
bloodbank issue A- 2
bloodbank register
```

### C Command-Line Design

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    if (argc == 3 && strcmp(argv[1], "find") == 0) {
        printf("Finding donors of blood group %s\n", argv[2]);
    }
    else if (argc == 4 && strcmp(argv[1], "issue") == 0) {
        char *end;
        long units = strtol(argv[3], &end, 10);

        if (*end != '\0' || units <= 0) {
            printf("Invalid number of units\n");
            return 1;
        }

        printf("Issuing %ld units of %s\n", units, argv[2]);
    }
    else {
        printf("Invalid command or arguments\n");
        printf("Usage:\n");
        printf("bloodbank find O+\n");
        printf("bloodbank issue A- 2\n");
        return 1;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

The complete program should also validate blood groups against the supported groups before processing a command.

### Handling Invalid Arguments

- If no arguments are given, display usage instructions.
- If too many arguments are given, display an error.
- If the blood group is invalid, reject the command.
- If the number of units is zero, negative or not numeric, reject it.

## Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis

Instead of returning a pointer, the function can return the index of the lowest-stock element.

### Alternative Function

```c
int findLowestIndex(Stock stock[], int count)
{
    int i;
    int minIndex = 0;

    if (count <= 0)
        return -1;

    for (i = 1; i < count; i++) {
        if (stock[i].units < stock[minIndex].units) {
            minIndex = i;
        }
    }

    return minIndex;
}
```

### Calling Code

```c
int index = findLowestIndex(stock, 8);

if (index != -1) {
    stock[index].units += 5;
}
```

### Comparison

| Feature | Pointer version | Index version |
|---|---|---|
| Return value | Address of element | Array index |
| Time complexity | O(n) | O(n) |
| Extra space | O(1) | O(1) |
| Updating | Direct through pointer | Access using index |
| Reallocation safety | Pointer may become invalid | Index can be used again with the new array |

### Conclusion

Both approaches require O(n) time and O(1) extra space. Returning an index is generally safer when a dynamic array may be reallocated because an old pointer can become invalid after `realloc()`. The index can be used with the new array address.
